# Securing WordPress: Redirecting Non-Authenticated Users to Login

Enforcing authentication on a WordPress site requires more than just checking if a user is logged in; it demands understanding how WordPress handles session redirects and how `wp-login.php` determines the destination URL upon successful login. When attempting to lock down a local or production instance so that only authenticated users can view content, developers often encounter a specific friction point: the default behavior of redirecting back to `wp-admin` instead of the requested page.

The core issue arises when you conditionally redirect unauthenticated users away from the homepage (`index.php`) to the login URL. The code snippet provided in the scenario attempts this using a header modification block within `header.php`:

```php
if (get_permalink() != wp_login_url() && !is_user_logged_in()) {
    wp_redirect( wp_login_url() ); exit;
}
```

While this logic correctly identifies non-logged-in users on pages other than the login page, it fails to preserve context. `wp_redirect()` sends an HTTP 302 status code and halts execution with `exit`. However, simply passing the current URL isn't enough if you want a seamless experience upon return. The critical failure mode here is not the redirection itself, but what happens after the user authenticates. By default, WordPress hooks into the login process to redirect users back to `wp-admin/admin-ajax.php` or `wp-admin` by default if no specific intent is captured in the session variable before the login action completes.

To solve this, you must ensure that the destination URL is explicitly passed to the authentication handler. The working alternative shown involves replacing the hard redirect with a link:

```php
if (get_permalink() != wp_login_url() && !is_user_logged_in()) {
    // Logic removed for now
}
<a href="<?php echo esc_url(wp_login_url( get_permalink() )); ?>" title="Login">Login</a>
```

This approach uses `wp_login_url( $redirect_to )`. This function accepts a URL argument and stores it in the session variable `$_SESSION['auth_redirect']` (or via cookies in older versions). When the user submits their credentials on the login page, WordPress processes the authentication and checks this stored value. If present, it redirects the user there instead of the default admin dashboard.

If you prefer to enforce a hard redirect rather than displaying a login button, you must ensure the `wp_login_url()` call includes the current page URL. The corrected logic for an aggressive redirect in your theme's `header.php` would look like this:

```php
if ( ! is_user_logged_in() && ! wp_is_json_request() ) {
    $redirect_url = home_url( add_query_arg( '', get_permalink() ) );
    // Ensure we don't redirect if we are already on the login page
    if ( strpos( $redirect_url, 'wp-login' ) === false ) {
        wp_redirect( wp_login_url( $redirect_url ) );
        exit;
    }
}
```

By passing `$redirect_url` to `wp_login_url()`, you are instructing WordPress: "If this user logs in successfully, send them here." This bypasses the default behavior of sending them to the admin panel. It is crucial to verify that `home_url()` matches your site's root path (e.g., `http://localhost/wordpress`) rather than just the domain root, as mismatched paths can cause 404 errors post-login.

Additionally, be mindful of the `wp_is_json_request()` check. If you are building a REST API client or using an AJAX loader that requires authentication before the page renders, a full-page redirect will break the JSON response. In such cases, handling authentication via headers or middleware (like in Laravel) is preferable to relying on `wp_redirect`.

For production environments where you might be migrating from a local setup to a shared host, consider using a plugin like "Force Login" if modifying theme files feels too fragile. However, understanding the underlying `wp_login_url` parameter ensures you retain full control over the user experience without dependency on third-party plugins that may introduce security vulnerabilities or outdated code.
